Experiments were conducted using GPT-2, OPT-1.3B, and LLaMA-7B language models for text generation, employing both Top-K sampling (K=20) and Beam search (beam width=8) decoding methods. The datasets used were C4 and Dbpedia Class. For evaluation, 500 human-written texts and 500 LLM-generated texts (each 200 
 5 tokens long, from 30-word prompts) were used to measure False Positive Rate (FPR), False Negative Rate (FNR), and F1 score. The proposed network-based UPV method was compared against a key-based z-score computation method (Kirchenbauer et al., 2023). Default hyperparameters included a watermark token ratio (
) of 0.5, a window size (
) of 5, 5 token embedding layers, and a watermark strength (
) of 2. The detector comprised two LSTM layers. Attack methods tested for unforgeability included reverse training of the generation network from the detection network and direct cracking based on token frequency analysis. Text quality was assessed by perplexity (PPL) using the LLaMA 13B model. Robustness to text rewriting was evaluated using GPT-3.5 turbo.

